WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.537
Welcome to Part 2 of the implementation
series for non-person identity.

2
00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.605
That means every identity
that isn't a person,

3
00:00:07.605 --> 00:00:10.425
from an old service
account to an AI agent.

4
00:00:10.425 --> 00:00:14.627
Part 1 listed every one of them,
gave each an owner and a sponsor,

5
00:00:14.627 --> 00:00:18.500
decided where each one goes,
and set the credential rules.

6
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.050
Part 2 covers the last three gates.

7
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.913
First, cutting privilege.

8
00:00:22.913 --> 00:00:27.025
Then converting each identity to
its target, with a parallel run.

9
00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:31.275
Then running the lifecycle, from an
owner change to a clean decommission.

10
00:00:31.275 --> 00:00:37.113
After that come SQL Server and AI agents,
and finally the evidence and the rollback.

11
00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:39.237
The format is the same as Part 1.

12
00:00:39.237 --> 00:00:43.980
Each gate gets its steps, the workbook's
lab commands with the kit's real output,

13
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.351
the federal requirement and evidence,

14
00:00:46.351 --> 00:00:50.649
availability in the government clouds,
an interim path, and a rollback.

15
00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.474
Design guidance is labeled,

16
00:00:52.474 --> 00:00:56.560
and the code is tested against
fixtures, not a live tenant.

17
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.848
Here are Part 2's three gates on one page.

18
00:01:02.848 --> 00:01:07.609
The least privilege gate is done when
every high-privilege grant is justified or

19
00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:10.381
removed, and every Tier
1 app registered in your

20
00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:13.509
own tenant has a workload
Conditional Access policy.

21
00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:18.158
The conversion gate is done when converted
identities sign in without stored

22
00:01:18.158 --> 00:01:22.448
secrets, and the legacy accounts are
disabled after a clean parallel run.

23
00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:25.988
The lifecycle gate is done
when the owner change, orphan,

24
00:01:25.988 --> 00:01:29.836
review and decommission paths
have all run and left records.

25
00:01:29.836 --> 00:01:34.310
Each gate names the read-only kit checks
that prove it, and each has a rollback.

26
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.135
Together they finish the track.

27
00:01:36.135 --> 00:01:40.910
Every identity has an owner, a
sponsor, a reason and a target.

28
00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.957
Each one is retired, moved
to a managed identity,

29
00:01:43.957 --> 00:01:47.160
or given a certificate or
a federated credential.

30
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.210
And none holds a password that never
expires, or is left without an owner.

31
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.563
Least privilege applies to software too,

32
00:01:58.563 --> 00:02:02.085
and a workload can use its
access faster than any person.

33
00:02:02.085 --> 00:02:04.797
The guide cuts privilege in five places.

34
00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:06.747
First, Graph permissions.

35
00:02:06.747 --> 00:02:10.073
Prefer delegated permissions
where a user is present.

36
00:02:10.073 --> 00:02:12.662
Judge each grant by
its type, not its name,

37
00:02:12.662 --> 00:02:16.585
because Mail Send as an
application permission is critical.

38
00:02:16.585 --> 00:02:19.760
An application permission
reaches across the whole tenant.

39
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.331
And removing a permission from
the app doesn't revoke the grant,

40
00:02:23.331 --> 00:02:25.460
so revoke it on the enterprise app too.

41
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.460
Second, directory roles.

42
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.110
No standing privileged role
unless the record justifies it.

43
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.548
A privileged service principal
is a control-plane asset.

44
00:02:34.548 --> 00:02:39.423
Third, Azure roles, scoped to
the one resource a workload uses.

45
00:02:39.423 --> 00:02:44.710
Fourth, custom security attributes, so
Conditional Access can pick apps by fact.

46
00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.473
Fifth, workload Conditional
Access, with real limits.

47
00:02:48.473 --> 00:02:50.284
Block is the only control.

48
00:02:50.284 --> 00:02:54.347
It covers only single-tenant
apps, never managed identities,

49
00:02:54.347 --> 00:02:57.272
and only when each one
is assigned directly.

50
00:02:57.272 --> 00:02:59.930
And new grants go through
the tiered approval.

51
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.992
The workbook's seventh
chapter sweeps every grant.

52
00:03:06.992 --> 00:03:09.322
It reads app roles, delegated grants,

53
00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:13.205
directory roles and Azure roles
for each service principal.

54
00:03:13.205 --> 00:03:16.162
On the fictional estate
it finds eleven service

55
00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:19.267
principals and twenty
grants, with seven findings.

56
00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:22.456
Three can grant themselves
more, or hold an admin role,

57
00:03:22.456 --> 00:03:25.355
so they go to the Privileged
Access plan instead.

58
00:03:25.355 --> 00:03:30.167
The plan has three writes: two app
role removals and one narrowed grant.

59
00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:33.339
Each removal goes through
the resource, never the app,

60
00:03:33.339 --> 00:03:36.943
and nothing is sent until a
reviewer approves the plan's hash.

61
00:03:36.943 --> 00:03:41.992
The eighth chapter keeps the two workload
policies as reviewed files, one per ring.

62
00:03:41.992 --> 00:03:45.030
Every policy is created
in report-only mode.

63
00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.805
Then it reads the sign-ins.

64
00:03:46.805 --> 00:03:47.968
Ring one is ready.

65
00:03:47.968 --> 00:03:52.817
Ring two isn't, because one workload
signed in from outside its known networks.

66
00:03:52.817 --> 00:03:57.590
Enforcement waits for the CISO's
decision and a reviewed sign-in summary.

67
00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:06.416
On the federal side, least privilege and
the review of privileged accounts call for

68
00:04:06.416 --> 00:04:09.726
the kit runs, with a justification
for every high grant,

69
00:04:09.726 --> 00:04:12.227
and the results of the PIM access reviews.

70
00:04:12.227 --> 00:04:16.592
CISA's baseline requires that only
administrators register apps,

71
00:04:16.592 --> 00:04:19.787
that user consent is
restricted, and that an admin

72
00:04:19.787 --> 00:04:23.528
consent workflow is in place,
so keep the ScubaGear report.

73
00:04:23.528 --> 00:04:26.153
To validate, run three kit checks.

74
00:04:26.153 --> 00:04:31.203
One lists every high-privilege grant,
and each must be justified or removed.

75
00:04:31.203 --> 00:04:35.875
One lists single-tenant apps
outside a directly assigned policy,

76
00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:37.778
and no Tier 1 app may be on it.

77
00:04:37.778 --> 00:04:41.202
One looks for managed identities
with broad Azure roles.

78
00:04:41.202 --> 00:04:44.482
Workload ID Premium is
available in Azure Government

79
00:04:44.482 --> 00:04:47.615
through the account manager,
but its free trial isn't.

80
00:04:47.615 --> 00:04:52.003
Without Premium, existing policies
keep working but can't be edited.

81
00:04:52.003 --> 00:04:55.058
To roll back, set the
policy back to report-only,

82
00:04:55.058 --> 00:04:58.470
and restore a removed
grant through approval.

83
00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.115
Conversion follows the
fourth phase of the Federal

84
00:05:05.115 --> 00:05:07.808
Modernization Workbook's
directory migration.

85
00:05:07.808 --> 00:05:12.168
It takes six to eight weeks, and the
order is a managed identity first,

86
00:05:12.168 --> 00:05:15.283
then a federated credential,
then a certificate.

87
00:05:15.283 --> 00:05:17.221
Step one builds the target.

88
00:05:17.221 --> 00:05:21.457
A federated credential must be
tested with a real token exchange.

89
00:05:21.457 --> 00:05:25.295
Step two names two human owners
and a rotation schedule.

90
00:05:25.295 --> 00:05:29.270
Step three runs old and new
side by side for thirty days.

91
00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:33.807
The application uses the new identity,
and the old account stays enabled.

92
00:05:33.807 --> 00:05:36.357
Watch the new identity's
sign-ins in Entra,

93
00:05:36.357 --> 00:05:39.732
and the old account's logons
on the domain controllers.

94
00:05:39.732 --> 00:05:43.332
Step four disables the old
account, but never deletes it.

95
00:05:43.332 --> 00:05:46.683
An Active Directory account
moves to a holding container.

96
00:05:46.683 --> 00:05:51.358
A secret is removed only after the new
credential has carried real traffic.

97
00:05:51.358 --> 00:05:57.240
Step five records the result: the target,
the date, and the disabled object's ID.

98
00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:05.465
A federated credential with the wrong
subject is created without any error.

99
00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:08.840
It fails only later, when
the token exchange runs.

100
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.262
So the workbook's fifth chapter
tests every exchange before cutover,

101
00:06:13.262 --> 00:06:15.202
against a fake token endpoint.

102
00:06:15.202 --> 00:06:17.039
Here two of six tests fail.

103
00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:20.865
One credential copied a sample's
issuer with a trailing slash,

104
00:06:20.865 --> 00:06:23.190
and wrote Production with a capital P.

105
00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.978
Another copied an audience
with a leading space.

106
00:06:25.978 --> 00:06:29.016
The conversion records
track each parallel run.

107
00:06:29.016 --> 00:06:32.501
One has run forty days cleanly,
and its owner approved,

108
00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:34.640
so it's the one disable in the plan.

109
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.352
Another is on day fifteen.

110
00:06:36.352 --> 00:06:40.765
A third still signed in with its old
secret eight weeks after cutover.

111
00:06:40.765 --> 00:06:43.665
The sixth chapter plans
the Kerberos accounts.

112
00:06:43.665 --> 00:06:48.721
It creates group managed service accounts,
completes one migration, and builds

113
00:06:48.721 --> 00:06:53.540
the island list of accounts that stay,
each with an owner and an end date.

114
00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:02.198
On the federal side, authenticator management
calls for the kit runs on expiring

115
00:07:02.198 --> 00:07:07.253
secrets and secret use, the app management
policy export, and the rotation calendar.

116
00:07:07.253 --> 00:07:11.023
Service identification calls
for each workload's permission

117
00:07:11.023 --> 00:07:14.577
grants and its sign-in log, which
also shows the parallel run.

118
00:07:14.577 --> 00:07:18.982
The workbook adds three tests: no
stored secrets where possible,

119
00:07:18.982 --> 00:07:22.889
no outage during the run, and
workload policies enforced.

120
00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:25.938
To validate, one kit check
confirms no high-risk

121
00:07:25.938 --> 00:07:29.065
account still has a password
that never expires.

122
00:07:29.065 --> 00:07:32.815
Another confirms the converted
apps no longer hold secrets.

123
00:07:32.815 --> 00:07:35.625
Creating a federated
credential and reading

124
00:07:35.625 --> 00:07:38.140
sign-ins work in the US Government clouds.

125
00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:42.853
A delegated managed service
account needs Windows Server 2025.

126
00:07:42.853 --> 00:07:46.679
Until an account can move, it
stays on the Kerberos island,

127
00:07:46.679 --> 00:07:48.552
with an owner and an end date.

128
00:07:48.552 --> 00:07:52.227
To roll back, re-enable the
old account and revert the app.

129
00:07:52.227 --> 00:07:55.480
That stays possible until
the account is deleted.

130
00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:02.079
A service account's events
come from its owning

131
00:08:02.079 --> 00:08:04.855
system and from credential
expiry, not from HR.

132
00:08:04.855 --> 00:08:06.780
The one exception is the sponsor.

133
00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:09.074
When an owner leaves or changes roles,

134
00:08:09.074 --> 00:08:12.330
the transfer starts at least
ten business days ahead.

135
00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:16.209
Make it a step in that person's
HR mover and leaver workflows,

136
00:08:16.209 --> 00:08:18.030
so nobody has to remember it.

137
00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.580
An app with no named owner is an orphan.

138
00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.857
It becomes Tier 1, the
governance office takes it over,

139
00:08:23.857 --> 00:08:27.655
and there are thirty days to find
an owner or start retiring it.

140
00:08:27.655 --> 00:08:30.655
On day thirty-one, access is suspended.

141
00:08:30.655 --> 00:08:34.581
An AI agent is disabled as soon
as the finding is confirmed,

142
00:08:34.581 --> 00:08:36.217
because an agent can act.

143
00:08:36.217 --> 00:08:38.732
Inactivity is a second orphan signal,

144
00:08:38.732 --> 00:08:42.868
from a preview report of each
service principal's last sign-in.

145
00:08:42.868 --> 00:08:46.256
And owners review their app's
permissions every quarter,

146
00:08:46.256 --> 00:08:49.420
with at least two owners
per app where possible.

147
00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:58.795
Retirement always disables first, so
nothing breaks silently, and deletes later.

148
00:08:58.795 --> 00:09:00.645
The mechanics differ by type.

149
00:09:00.645 --> 00:09:03.059
An Active Directory account is disabled,

150
00:09:03.059 --> 00:09:07.745
and its service principal names are
removed only after the observation period.

151
00:09:07.745 --> 00:09:10.207
An app registration is deactivated.

152
00:09:10.207 --> 00:09:15.507
New tokens stop at once, but tokens already
issued stay valid until they expire.

153
00:09:15.507 --> 00:09:18.395
A deleted app can be
restored for thirty days.

154
00:09:18.395 --> 00:09:21.933
A service principal is blocked
from signing in to this tenant.

155
00:09:21.933 --> 00:09:26.732
If it's deleted and restored, its
Conditional Access policies don't come back.

156
00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:30.252
A managed identity loses
its role assignments first,

157
00:09:30.252 --> 00:09:32.732
and once deleted, it can't be recovered.

158
00:09:32.732 --> 00:09:36.420
An agent can be disabled by its
sponsor, but not re-enabled.

159
00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.220
Deleting a blueprint
removes its agents too.

160
00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.170
Every deactivation shows in the audit log.

161
00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:44.470
File that entry with the decision.

162
00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:52.195
The workbook's tenth chapter turns
this gate into a scheduled run.

163
00:09:52.195 --> 00:09:57.458
Each run compares every identity with a
baseline that someone approved by its hash.

164
00:09:57.458 --> 00:10:02.038
On the second fixture day it finds
seven ownerless orphan candidates,

165
00:10:02.038 --> 00:10:04.645
and two apps unused for over ninety days.

166
00:10:04.645 --> 00:10:07.910
It finds three accounts
whose password never expires,

167
00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:10.957
and three credentials
added outside the pipeline.

168
00:10:10.957 --> 00:10:15.471
A credential counts as the pipeline's
only when two separate records agree.

169
00:10:15.471 --> 00:10:20.433
The review check finds an app whose last
owner review was in May, too long ago.

170
00:10:20.433 --> 00:10:25.771
Then each run is packaged with a checksum
for every file, chained to the day before.

171
00:10:25.771 --> 00:10:29.308
So a swapped package or a
missing day shows up as a break.

172
00:10:29.308 --> 00:10:32.783
The period report is
what the ISSO receives.

173
00:10:32.783 --> 00:10:36.607
Here the chain is unbroken,
but the period isn't complete.

174
00:10:36.607 --> 00:10:40.450
Orphans and passwords that
never expire are still open.

175
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:48.544
On the federal side, account management
calls for the identity record, the

176
00:10:48.544 --> 00:10:50.894
owner-change and decommission tickets,

177
00:10:50.894 --> 00:10:53.600
and the kit runs for
owners and agent sponsors.

178
00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:58.863
Identifier management calls for a record
keyed by object ID, never reused.

179
00:10:58.863 --> 00:11:02.501
Audit controls call for the
logs routed out of Entra ID,

180
00:11:02.501 --> 00:11:06.463
including every credential
added and every deactivation.

181
00:11:06.463 --> 00:11:10.299
To validate, run the owner
and expiry checks every week.

182
00:11:10.299 --> 00:11:14.200
A new app with fewer than two
owners opens a ticket that week.

183
00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:18.288
No agent may go past ten business
days without a confirmed sponsor.

184
00:11:18.288 --> 00:11:22.575
An optional preview check lists service
principals idle for ninety days.

185
00:11:22.575 --> 00:11:27.275
Then run one test owner change and
one test decommission, end to end.

186
00:11:27.275 --> 00:11:32.411
The sign-in activity report is listed
only for the commercial service,

187
00:11:32.411 --> 00:11:34.713
so GCC has it, and GCC High doesn't.

188
00:11:34.713 --> 00:11:38.005
To roll back, reactivate,
or restore within thirty

189
00:11:38.005 --> 00:11:42.050
days and re-create the
Conditional Access assignments.

190
00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.925
SQL Server follows the same logic,
with three kinds of principal.

191
00:11:49.925 --> 00:11:54.400
The engine's service account works only
while Active Directory is in charge.

192
00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.200
Azure Arc doesn't replace it.

193
00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.363
It only changes what
Entra ID and Azure see.

194
00:11:59.363 --> 00:12:02.688
The instance's own Entra
identity is a managed

195
00:12:02.688 --> 00:12:06.801
identity on SQL Server 2025,
with no credential at all.

196
00:12:06.801 --> 00:12:11.803
On SQL Server 2022 it's an app
registration with a Key Vault certificate,

197
00:12:11.803 --> 00:12:14.063
which needs a named renewal owner.

198
00:12:14.063 --> 00:12:19.716
Application logins move from SQL passwords
to Entra logins: a managed identity

199
00:12:19.716 --> 00:12:24.387
first, then a certificate, and a
secret only as a documented exception.

200
00:12:24.387 --> 00:12:27.225
And the same thirty-day
parallel run applies.

201
00:12:27.225 --> 00:12:28.737
Two dangers stand out.

202
00:12:28.737 --> 00:12:34.075
A renewed certificate that nobody uploaded
fails at expiry, so automate the upload.

203
00:12:34.075 --> 00:12:38.781
And on an Arc server, every allowed
process presents the same identity,

204
00:12:38.781 --> 00:12:42.880
so treat one login per server
as the unit of attribution.

205
00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:48.326
AI agents follow this guide,

206
00:12:48.326 --> 00:12:52.530
with a few additions from the
workbook's AI Identity Governance book.

207
00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:55.168
Every agent needs a
sponsor when it's created.

208
00:12:55.168 --> 00:12:57.353
The blueprint holds the credentials,

209
00:12:57.353 --> 00:13:01.018
so one action on the blueprint
reaches every agent made from it.

210
00:13:01.018 --> 00:13:03.588
Ordinary users can't create blueprints,

211
00:13:03.588 --> 00:13:07.481
and a production handshake comes
before any agent goes live.

212
00:13:07.481 --> 00:13:10.462
No production blueprint holds a secret,

213
00:13:10.462 --> 00:13:13.805
and every API key is
recorded as an exception.

214
00:13:13.805 --> 00:13:16.576
Access comes through
packages with end dates,

215
00:13:16.576 --> 00:13:19.493
so a sponsor who does
nothing lets access expire.

216
00:13:19.493 --> 00:13:24.229
The agent platform has been
generally available since April 2026,

217
00:13:24.229 --> 00:13:26.209
with a partial subset in GCC.

218
00:13:26.209 --> 00:13:29.481
Agent Conditional Access
is still in preview.

219
00:13:29.481 --> 00:13:32.357
The workbook's ninth
chapter checks all of this,

220
00:13:32.357 --> 00:13:36.193
and here it finds twenty-two
findings, four of them top priority.

221
00:13:36.193 --> 00:13:40.183
For GCC High and DoD, Microsoft
has published nothing,

222
00:13:40.183 --> 00:13:44.360
so govern the service
principals the agents run as.

223
00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:52.184
Across the whole track, the
evidence covers account management,

224
00:13:52.184 --> 00:13:57.410
least privilege, identifiers, authenticators,
service identification and audit.

225
00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:01.993
It's the record, the tickets, the
kit runs with their justifications,

226
00:14:01.993 --> 00:14:05.910
the access review results, the
policy export, and the logs.

227
00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:08.423
Two CISA items sit across the track.

228
00:14:08.423 --> 00:14:11.437
ScubaGear also checks that
apps have no passwords,

229
00:14:11.437 --> 00:14:13.948
and that credential lifetimes are capped.

230
00:14:13.948 --> 00:14:17.187
And it marks blocking risky
AI agents as required,

231
00:14:17.187 --> 00:14:20.822
though CISA's own list didn't
include it in late October.

232
00:14:20.822 --> 00:14:23.280
So record it now, and
enforce it when agent

233
00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.810
risk is generally available in your cloud.

234
00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:28.344
Run the checks on the monitoring cadence,

235
00:14:28.344 --> 00:14:31.773
and file each run with its
date, tenant and check version.

236
00:14:31.773 --> 00:14:34.285
Rollback is defined for every step.

237
00:14:34.285 --> 00:14:39.210
Part 1's steps roll back by re-deciding,
or by an exception with an end date.

238
00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.697
Least privilege goes back to report-only.

239
00:14:41.697 --> 00:14:44.123
Conversion re-enables the old account.

240
00:14:44.123 --> 00:14:48.820
And decommissioning reactivates,
or restores within thirty days.

241
00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:54.332
Keep five dangers in view.

242
00:14:54.332 --> 00:14:57.619
Every app owner can add a
credential and act as the app,

243
00:14:57.619 --> 00:15:00.607
so keep owners to the two
people who configure it.

244
00:15:00.607 --> 00:15:04.735
Disabled isn't revoked, because
issued tokens stay valid,

245
00:15:04.735 --> 00:15:08.071
so in an incident, also
remove the credentials.

246
00:15:08.071 --> 00:15:12.982
A privileged service principal is a
control-plane asset, not an ordinary app.

247
00:15:12.982 --> 00:15:17.040
Workload Conditional Access
silently skips managed identities,

248
00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.507
multitenant apps, and anything
assigned through a group.

249
00:15:20.507 --> 00:15:24.102
And by default, whoever builds
an agent becomes its sponsor,

250
00:15:24.102 --> 00:15:27.696
so close blueprint creation
and set the sponsor on purpose.

251
00:15:27.696 --> 00:15:32.833
Where to go next: Part 1 covers inventory,
decisions and credential rules.

252
00:15:32.833 --> 00:15:36.473
The developer workbook,
Building Non-Person Identity,

253
00:15:36.473 --> 00:15:40.357
has chapters five to ten for
these gates, each with its labs.

254
00:15:40.357 --> 00:15:42.244
The Labs page lists them all.

255
00:15:42.244 --> 00:15:44.408
A person leaves and HR tells you.

256
00:15:44.408 --> 00:15:47.408
A service account's owner
leaves and nothing tells you.

257
00:15:47.408 --> 00:15:49.660
These gates build the record that does.
